<Docs
  title="Quota ledger"
  description="The local record behind the quota-aware agent pool, its persisted ledger and availability states."
  toc={toc}
>
  <Callout tone="note" title="Slot persistence and the ledger are implemented">
    The pool slot record, its local database table and the append-only quota ledger now exist,
    together with the six availability states. Priority selection, idle release and the pool UI are
    still planned, so the agent pool is not available in the current build. This page records the
    agreed contract so the implementation, tests and user guide cannot drift.
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="status">Status</h2>
  <p>
    <Badge tone="warning" variant="subtle">planned</Badge> The pool work is split into slot
    persistence, the quota ledger, priority selection, idle release and the pool UI. Slot
    persistence and the quota ledger are implemented and tested; priority selection, idle release
    and the pool UI are not checked off. The user-facing promise is on the
    <a href="/docs/users/agent-pool">agent pool</a> page; this page is for the implementation.
  </p>

  <h2 id="slot">The slot record</h2>
  <p>
    A slot is an installed agent plus one provider/model plus the credential or account it uses. A
    persisted slot needs:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The user's priority order and the provider/model metadata.</li>
    <li>A local usage ledger, reset window, cooldown state and last-used timestamp.</li>
    <li>A reference to the credential or account. Never copy the credential into the slot, logs or database.</li>
    <li>An enabled/disabled state and the last error or health signal.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="ledger">The ledger</h2>
  <p>
    The ledger is local and append-only. Each observation records its source (catalog, provider or
    user), timestamp, used and limit values, unit, reset time, cooldown and error. A later
    observation, including a user correction, is the current one. The ledger is not a provider
    billing record and must not claim a limit it cannot observe.
  </p>
  <p>
    A quota response is evidence for a slot, not a global fact. The source and timestamp are
    preserved so a later reset or correction can be explained. If the provider reports no capacity,
    the observation leaves the usage or limit unknown; never translate that to free or unlimited.
  </p>

  <h2 id="availability">Availability</h2>
  <p>
    Before selection, a slot answers whether it can accept work at the current time. The derived
    states are available, exhausted, cooling down, disabled, unhealthy and unknown. A disabled slot
    wins first; an active cooldown, then a recorded error, then an expired reset window are checked
    before the used/limit comparison. Expired reset periods make a known limit available again;
    unknown usage or limits stay unknown. Exhausted and cooling-down slots are skipped with a visible reason,
    and unknown capacity is not treated as available.
  </p>

  <h2 id="selection">Selection</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Try the user's preferred healthy slot.</li>
    <li>If it cannot accept work, try the next slot in priority order.</li>
    <li>An explicit task override wins over the order.</li>
    <li>Record the chosen slot and every skipped higher-priority slot with its reason.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Selection must be atomic across concurrent tasks. The transcript or task header names the chosen
    slot, so a fallback is never silent. A slot that is exhausted for one provider/model must not
    block an unrelated slot.
  </p>

  <h2 id="release">Idle release</h2>
  <p>
    After a turn settles, keep the slot reserved for a configurable idle timeout (default 15
    minutes). Release it after that timeout so another task can use it. Never release during a
    running turn or a pending approval.
  </p>
  <p>
    Releasing a reservation must not destroy a resumable agent session. If the adapter cannot resume,
    the task says so and selects another slot for the next turn. Tests need a follow-up after release,
    a slot claimed in the meantime, cancellation and restart recovery.
  </p>

  <h2 id="privacy">Corrections and privacy</h2>
  <p>
    The user can edit a limit or reset time, clear a cooldown, reorder slots, disable a slot and force
    a slot for one turn. The pool UI shows provider/model, priority, availability, known limit/reset,
    current task, last error and last-used time. It must not display credentials or invent a quota.
  </p>
  <p>
    The catalog is advisory. A local ledger and the user's correction are authoritative for local
    selection; a stale catalog snapshot must not silently overwrite them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tests">Tests</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Implemented: ledger round-trips both stores, ordering, re-open durability, owner checks and rejected invalid input.</li>
    <li>Implemented: a deterministic clock covers reset windows, cooldowns, provider errors, clock changes, user corrections and every availability state.</li>
    <li>Pending: simultaneous starts, manual override, idle release, a slot claimed meanwhile, cancellation and restart recovery.</li>
    <li>Credential redaction in records, logs and diagnostics.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The selection and release tests arrive with those slices, so the agent pool remains a next-step
    item. See the <a href="/docs/contributors/test-fixtures">test fixtures</a> page for the fixture
    rules that apply when the work lands.
  </p>
</Docs>
